  "text": "Here is the step-by-step solution to find the area and perimeter of the outer boundary, as well as the area of the path itself.\n\n### Step 1: Determine the dimensions of the inner rectangle\nThe problem states that the inner rectangle has dimensions $4$ by $7$.\n*   Height ($h_{inner}$) = $4$\n*   Width ($w_{inner}$) = $7$\n\n### Step 2: Determine the dimensions of the outer boundary\nA path of width $1$ runs around the **outside** of the rectangle. This means we add $1$ unit to each side (left, right, top, and bottom).\n\n*   **New Height**: The path adds $1$ unit to the top and $1$ unit to the bottom.\n    $$h_{outer} = h_{inner} + 1 + 1 = 4 + 2 = 6$$\n\n*   **New Width**: The path adds $1$ unit to the left and $1$ unit to the right.\n    $$w_{outer} = w_{inner} + 1 + 1 = 7 + 2 = 9$$\n\nSo, the outer boundary forms a rectangle with dimensions $6$ by $9$.\n\n### Step 3: Calculate the Area of the Outer Boundary\nThe area of a rectangle is calculated as $\\text{height} \\times \\text{width}$.\n\n$$\\text{Area}_{outer} = h_{outer} \\times w_{outer}$$\n$$\\text{Area}_{outer} = 6 \\times 9 = 54$$\n\n### Step 4: Calculate the Perimeter of the Outer Boundary\nThe perimeter of a rectangle is calculated as $2 \\times (\\text{height} + \\text{width})$.\n\n$$\\text{Perimeter}_{outer} = 2 \\times (h_{outer} + w_{outer})$$\n$$\\text{Perimeter}_{outer} = 2 \\times (6 + 9)$$\n$$\\text{Perimeter}_{outer} = 2 \\times 15 = 30$$\n\n### Step 5: Calculate the Area of the Path Alone\nThe area of the path is the difference between the area of the outer boundary and the area of the inner rectangle.\n\nFirst, calculate the area of the inner rectangle:\n$$\\text{Area}_{inner} = 4 \\times 7 = 28$$\n\nNow, subtract the inner area from the outer area:\n$$\\text{Area}_{path} = \\text{Area}_{outer} - \\text{Area}_{inner}$$\n$$\\text{Area}_{path} = 54 - 28 = 26$$\n\n### Summary\n*   **Area of the outer boundary**: $54$\n*   **Perimeter of the outer boundary**: $30$\n*   **Area of the path alone**: $26$\n\nAnswer: 54, 30, 26",
